  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study reports safety outcomes after sequential knowledge editing, extending the question of data-driven safety degradation to a different parameter-update setting than fine-tuning.",
    "gate_note": "The study measures unsafe-response rates after model editing, but does not test fine-tuning, response-position KL, compliant-token prefilling, suffix or decoding bypasses, safety localization in a small set of parameters, or methods for deepening safety. Included rows concern model-editing outcomes and are linked to no listed version.",
    "test_reason": "The study measures unsafe-response rates (ASR) after locate-then-edit knowledge editing (ROME, MEMIT, NSE, AlphaEdit, etc.), with malicious or benign edits. Every version names fine-tuning, per-token KL, opening bypasses, parameter localization, the cause of shallowness, or deepening training. Closed-form editing of a few FFN layers is not fine-tuning, so no row measures what a version names. The harmful-compliance increases are a related finding about a different operation and go under fits_no_version. The record's 'tests/T3' contradicts its own all-'none' row links and is corrected here.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct: sequential locate-then-edit editing raises safety risk with both malicious and benign edits, and SAME's safety-direction constraints mitigate this while preserving editing performance.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study performs parameter-modifying knowledge edits in a locate-then-edit framework, rather than fine-tuning examples or measuring response-token distribution differences. SAME derives safety directions from paired representations and applies them to activation and parameter updates.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported outcomes vary with editing method, malicious-data ratio, model, dataset, and number of sequential edits. AlphaEdit on Qwen3-4B-Instruct has a lower ASR at r = 50% than at r = 20% in Table 2; the text attributes this pattern in a related result to model degradation and vague safe responses.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Locate-then-edit with malicious affirmative-response targets raises ASR. For example, Qwen3-4B AlphaEdit goes from an unedited 9.7% to 53.6% at r=20%; Llama NSE on COUNTERFACT goes from 13.0% to 54.7% at r=50%.",
        "proposed_claim": "Knowledge editing with harmful targets degrades safety alignment of instruct models, analogous to harmful fine-tuning.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R2",
          "R3",
          "R5",
          "R6",
          "R8",
          "R9",
          "R17",
          "R18",
          "R19",
          "R20",
          "R21",
          "R22",
          "R25",
          "R26",
          "R27",
          "R28",
          "R29"
        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Many sequential benign factual edits modestly raise ASR for some methods. Llama AlphaEdit goes from 12.3 to 21.0 at 2000 edits; MEMIT on Qwen3-4B stays roughly flat (9.9 to 10.7).",
        "proposed_claim": "Large volumes of benign knowledge edits can erode safety, depending on method and model.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R13",
          "R14",
          "R15"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "Proceedings of the 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), 2026",
    "problems": "The study reports no uncertainty estimates for the ASR values. ASR uses a safety classifier, while harmful-score judgments use GPT-5; human validation is reported for harmful scores, not as uncertainty estimates for the ASR results. The authors note model collapse in some editing conditions.",
    "rows_left_out": "The study reports additional ASR results not extracted here, including other model and dataset combinations in Tables 1, 2, 8–10, sequential-edit results in Appendix D.1, and the multi-turn attack results in Table 15. They concern model editing rather than any listed claim version; the retained rows prioritize the explicitly discussed Qwen3-4B-Instruct COUNTERFACT ratios, the reported benign-edit example, and the Mal-KSet edit comparison.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No fine-tuning on harmful examples; malicious data is applied by locate-then-edit knowledge editing."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No benign fine-tuning; benign data are factual triple edits (COUNTERFACT, ZsRE)."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No per-position KL or token-depth analysis."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No prefilling, suffix or decoding attacks. The multi-turn Crescendo attack is on edited models and is not an opening-token test."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Edits touch a few FFN layers, but the study does not test whether safety lives in few weights. SafeDir is a representation direction used for the method."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Not tested."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "SAME is an editing constraint, not safety training that spreads safety beyond the opening tokens."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "Qwen2.5-14B results (Table 10), the multi-turn attacks (Table 15) and the Qwen sequential results (Table 9) are declared in rows_left_out. ASR is judged by a safety classifier and HS by GPT-5; no uncertainty is reported. The benign-edit effect is method-dependent (MEMIT on Qwen is nearly flat). If curators broaden V1/V2 to any weight update, the malicious-edit rows would support a V1 analogue in a new setting (T3), and the benign-edit rows would give a weak, method-dependent V2 analogue."
  },
